---
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro'
import PageHead from '@/components/PageHead.astro'
import TableOfContents from '@/components/TableOfContents.astro'
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro'

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'oss', text: 'Open Source Projects' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'gravidy', text: 'GraviDy' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'gadget-snapshot-reader', text: 'Gadget Snapshot Reader' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'research', text: 'Research Projects' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'black-hole-binaries', text: 'Numerical modelling of black hole binaries' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'globular-clusters', text: 'Dynamical evolution of globular clusters' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'medical-image-processing', text: 'Medical Image Processing' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'publications', text: 'Publications' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'publications-astro', text: 'Astrophysics' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'publications-cs', text: 'Computer Science' },
]
---

<Layout>
  <PageHead slot="head" title="Academia" />
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Academia', icon: 'lucide:info' }]} />

  <section>
    <div class="relative min-w-full">
      <h1 class="text-3xl mb-5">Academia</h1>
      <div class="prose">
      <p class="mb-4">
      My academic experience is a bit limited because I decided to pursue an
      industry job after I finished my doctoral studies, but still there are
      many projects, research and experiences that are worth highlighting here.
      </p>
      </div>

      <!--
        On xl the TOC lives in the empty margin left of the content column and
        takes no space in the flow, so the content keeps the same position and
        width as on other pages. The width fills that margin (the Layout's
        max-w-4xl is 56rem; its px-4 leaves room for the scrollbar), capped at 16rem.
      -->
      <div class="mb-6 xl:absolute xl:inset-y-0 xl:right-full xl:mb-0 xl:w-[min(16rem,calc((100vw-56rem)/2))]">
        <TableOfContents headings={headings} />
      </div>

      <div class="prose mb-8">
      <section>
      <h2 id="oss" class="text-2xl">Open Source Projects</h2>

      </section>
      <section>
      <h3 id="gravidy" class="text-xl">GraviDy</h3>
      <p class="mb-4">
      A direct-summation <em>N</em>-body integrator, which can be used on clusters/nodes with
      different flavours of parallelism (OpenMP, MPI or CUDA).
      It's based on the fourth-order Hermite scheme, but can be extended to other
      integration methods.
      </p>

      <ul class="text-base/5">
       <li>Web: <a href="https://gravidy.xyz">gravidy.xyz</a></li>
       <li>Repo: <a href="https://gitlab.com/cmaureir/gravidy">gitlab.com/cmaureir/gravidy</a></li>
      </ul>

      </section>
      <section>
      <h3 id="gadget-snapshot-reader" class="text-xl">Gadget Snapshot Reader</h3>
      <p class="mb-4">
      A simple Python module to read <a
      href="http://wwwmpa.mpa-garching.mpg.de/gadget/">Gadget</a> snapshots
      (type 1).
      </p>

      <ul>
      <li>Repo: <a href="https://github.com/cmaureir/gadget-snapshot-reader">https://github.com/cmaureir/gadget-snapshot-reader</a></li>
      </ul>

      </section>
      <section>
      <h2 id="research" class="text-2xl">Research Projects</h2>

      <p class="mb-4">
      Here you can find the ideas behind the research projects in which I have been involved.
      </p>

      </section>
      <section>
      <h3 id="black-hole-binaries" class="text-xl">Numerical modelling of black hole binaries</h3>
      <p class="mb-4">
      As this was the main topic of my PhD thesis, I was involved in a few projects
      that studied the dynamical evolution of black hole binaries in gas-rich environments,
      while also considering the gas properties and how we can get some information
      related to circumbinary structures.</p>
      <p>For this topic, I used <a href="https://www.h-its.org/tap-software-en/gadget-code/">Gadget</a>,
      which is a cosmological <em>N</em>-body/Smoothed Particle Hydrodynamics code.
      </p>

      <p class="mb-4">
      <strong>Collaborators</strong>: <em>F. Goicovic, L. del Valle, A. Sesana, M. Dotti, P. Amaro-Seoane</em>
      </p>

      </section>
      <section>
      <h3 id="globular-clusters" class="text-xl">Dynamical evolution of globular clusters</h3>
      <p class="mb-4">
      During my Master's, I decided to study astrophysical simulations of <em>N</em>-body systems,
      which became the first project for my PhD.
      I developed <a href="http://gravidy.xyz">GraviDy</a> to have a well-structured
      code as a base for future projects.
      </p>

      <p class="mb-4">
      <strong>Collaborators</strong>: <a href="https://astro-gr.org">P. Amaro-Seoane</a>
      </p>

      </section>
      <section>
      <h3 id="medical-image-processing" class="text-xl">Medical Image Processing</h3>
      <p class="mb-4">
      Immunohistochemistry (IHC) analysis is a common practice to evaluate the
      evolution of different types of cancer.
      This process needs a biomarker to identify the cell membrane,
      like the Human Epidermal growth factor Receptor 2 (HER2).
      To study these images, one can use different texture features at the pixel level
      to detect changes in the cells and classify them.
      </p>

      <p class="mb-4">
      I helped <a href="https://github.com/rpezoa"><em>Raquel Pezoa</em></a> on this project,
      adapting code that calculates the
      <a href="https://github.com/rpezoa/haralick">Haralick texture features</a> of these images,
      to run on large HPC clusters.
      </p>

      </section>
      <section>
